Switch: Aurora's sqlite caches with a persistent rollback journal, no VACUUM

The game on the console is usually closed with HOME, which kills the process. With the
previous journal_mode=MEMORY a commit (or the VACUUM after the Dawn cache prune, which
rewrites the whole file) cut short by the kill left dawn_cache.db damaged: the next run
failed every lookup ("database disk image is malformed"). Mac kill -9 test with the
Switch's sqlite build options (switch/aurora/sqlite_kill_test.c): 113 of 150 kills damaged
the file with MEMORY, 0 of 150 with PERSIST, TRUNCATE or DELETE.

On Horizon the caches use journal_mode=PERSIST (the -journal file is created once and its
header zeroed at each commit; with the build's exclusive locking it stays open),
journal_size_limit=4 MiB and synchronous=OFF (what write() handed the file system before a
kill still reaches the card; a commit cut short is rolled back from the journal at the
next open). COS_SWITCH_SQLITE_JOURNAL (env.txt) picks persist (default), truncate, delete
or memory. Each cache logs its journal mode once when it opens.

A journal that does not work falls back to journal_mode=MEMORY for the run, with one
line: at open, the header's user_version is rewritten with its own value, a write that
makes sqlite create the journal; and a write that fails later with an I/O error (or
CANTOPEN) switches the connection and is tried once more (sqlite::journal_fallback, called
by the shared patch 0007 for Dawn cache stores and prunes, the pipeline cache's seeding
and its writer thread). The first PERSIST build failed every write on the console with
SQLITE_IOERR_FSTAT: libnx cannot stat a file that is open for writing, which sqlite does
when it creates the journal; switch/aurora/sqlite_horizon.c now answers that stat.

The Dawn cache prune no longer VACUUMs on Horizon: that rewrote tens of MiB on the SD card
(twice with a journal) for seconds with cache_mutex held, so every Dawn lookup on the
render worker waited; freed pages are reused by later inserts.

A cache that cannot be opened, for any reason, is removed and created once more from an
empty file (the shared patch native/patches/aurora/0007 does the removal and retry, for
damaged files only on other platforms).

Switch only (switch/native/aurora/patches); not for upstream.

